From a392bacd989f9795c2847778a8d156fb83a50458 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: FreeKC <fredrenaud25@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 06:28:47 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] fix(indexer): never let a raised start ledger skip past
 the checkpoint

Fixes #45.

With a checkpoint present the next range began at
max(last_processed_ledger + 1, start_ledger), so a start ledger raised above
the checkpoint moved the range forward and left every ledger in between
unread, permanently and without a word. The range now resumes immediately
after the checkpoint whatever the start ledger says; the start ledger is
honoured on a first run only.

The raised value is reported rather than silently dropped, and both places an
operator can raise it are checked because they failed differently: the
checkpoint row's own start_ledger is what moved the range (the gap), while
INDEXER_START_LEDGER is not consulted once a checkpoint exists, which is just
as surprising to whoever raised it expecting a jump. Each source that is ahead
is logged with the ledgers at stake and returned in a `warnings` array of the
run's response, on every run until it agrees with the checkpoint again. The
documented rebuild (last_processed_ledger = start_ledger - 1) stays silent and
keeps working.

Tests cover the range computation, the gap classifier, and the handler for a
raised checkpoint column, a raised environment variable, the rebuild and the
caught-up path. The runbook gains a section on the behaviour.
